  "notes": "An insertion cell array, the concept of the reference design sorter_ins (par, record 7b878f6d1ea4) and of record 3b1ec4b16d7a, rebuilt around one comparator per cell: g[k] = in_valid && tok[k] && (in_data < data[k]), read by cell k (hold) and by cell k-1 (take the input). g is monotone over the sorted valid cells, so each cell's next value is a flat sum of three products: the value of the cell above when !g[k+1], the input when g[k+1] && !g[k], its own value when g[k]; the top cell takes the input unless g[N-1]. The token line marks the valid cells, so the data flops need no reset and no drain to zero: a cell whose token is 0 never enters g. The outputs are the bottom cell's flops (out_valid = tok[0], out_data = data[0]) with no copy register: 72 flops at N = 8 against 81. So every output comes one snapshot earlier than par's, at the snapshots s + N .. s + 2N - 1 after a start at entry s: latency 2N - 1, the floor the task names, and the next start may come one entry earlier too. That is the one behaviour that differs from par's and from 3b1ec4b16d7a's; the values, their order and the reset behave as theirs, and the outputs stay Moore (proved; in the netlist each output port is a flip-flop's Q). The design is parametric in N and W. The design and the proof are the player's files, byte for byte, both written by Google Antigravity CLI with the model Gemini 4 Neon, working on its own as an autonomous agent, in one commit. The proof file has 1204 lines (1107 of code, the board's count). It follows the route of par's proof sorter_ins.lean (the phase model of StreamTask.satisfiedAtV2_of_phases) and keeps its sorted-list lemmas: 68 of its 856 lines longer than 25 characters are lines of par's proof. The rest is its own: this design's mirror and clock cycle with the combinational g, the cell evaluation, the feed and drain invariants with the token as sentinel, and rule 1 for the continuous assigns. The maintainers wrote none of its lines.",
